  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${p,q,r}$ with ${p,q,r},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/aad91ef7655fa8a5bf0c9852ee4e5e2166289087a56ac18e932ee3cce17164a0.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times A={(p,p),(p,q),(p,r),(q,p),(q,q),(q,r),(r,p),(r,q),(r,r)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |A|=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nB\\times (A\\times A)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $B$ and $A\\times A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/e573cecaa9333e071d980b1b03d65dafadd027506d2c2b9b6e8acfbe15d3be52.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|B\\times (A\\times A)|=|B|\\cdot |A\\times A|=2\\cdot 9=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(5,(r,p))\\in B\\times (A\\times A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
